################################################################################
#                                                                              #
#   K A R O   —   e n v i r o n m e n t                                        #
#                                                                              #
#   COPY THIS FILE AND CHANGE NOTHING:                                         #
#                                                                              #
#       cp .env.example .env                                                   #
#       docker compose up -d                                                   #
#       npm run db:push && npm run db:seed && npm run dev                      #
#                                                                              #
#   That is a complete, working install. Every value below is either            #
#   already correct for local development or commented out, and every           #
#   integration that needs a real credential falls back to a mock when          #
#   the credential is absent. With no API keys at all, Karo runs in             #
#   DEMO MODE: the AI streams realistic scripted responses with tool            #
#   calls, the sandbox simulates a filesystem and a shell, and billing          #
#   simulates checkout. Nothing is stubbed out or disabled — the whole          #
#   product is usable.                                                         #
#                                                                              #
#   Adding a real credential switches that one integration on. You never        #
#   flip a second flag to enable something.                                    #
#                                                                              #
#   Every variable here is read by src/lib/env.ts. Anything not listed          #
#   there is ignored. NEVER commit a filled-in .env — .gitignore already        #
#   excludes it. The placeholder secrets below are deliberately fake.           #
#                                                                              #
################################################################################


# ==============================================================================
#  CORE
# ==============================================================================

# Runtime mode. `development` | `test` | `production`.
# Optional — defaults to `development`.
# In `production` the app REFUSES to start without ENCRYPTION_KEY; in
# development it falls back to an insecure dev value and warns once.
NODE_ENV=development

# Absolute public origin of this install. Used for email links, OAuth
# redirects, Stripe return URLs and absolute URLs in metadata.
# Optional — defaults to http://localhost:3000.
# Set this to your real https:// origin in production or verification
# emails will point at localhost.
APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Product name shown in the UI, page titles and outbound email.
# Optional — defaults to `Karo`. Change it for a white-label deployment.
APP_NAME=Karo


# ==============================================================================
#  DATABASE
# ==============================================================================

# PostgreSQL connection string.
# Optional — defaults to postgresql://karo:karo@localhost:5432/karo, which is
# exactly what `docker compose up -d` gives you.
# If Postgres is unreachable the app still boots; pages that read the
# database surface an error state instead of crashing the process.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://karo:karo@localhost:5432/karo

# Require TLS on the database connection.
# Optional — defaults to false. Accepts 1/true/yes/on.
# Turn it on for managed Postgres (Neon, Supabase, RDS). Leave it off for
# the local container, which has no certificate.
DATABASE_SSL=false

# Size of the postgres.js connection pool.
# Optional — defaults to 10.
# Keep the sum across all app instances below the server's max_connections
# (the production compose file sets 100).
DATABASE_MAX_CONNECTIONS=10


# ==============================================================================
#  REDIS
# ==============================================================================

# Redis connection string.
# Optional — no default.
# WHEN ABSENT: rate limiting, the session cache and the SSE fan-out fall
# back to a per-process in-memory implementation. That is correct for a
# single dev process and WRONG for more than one production instance —
# counters would be per-instance, so a rate limit of 60/min effectively
# becomes 60/min *per container*. Set it in production.
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379


# ==============================================================================
#  SECRETS
#
#  There is exactly one. Generate it before deploying; the command prints one
#  line, paste it verbatim, quotes are not needed.
#
#      ENCRYPTION_KEY   openssl rand -base64 32
#
#  No openssl (Windows)? Node ships the same primitive:
#
#      node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
#
#  `AUTH_SECRET` and `BYOS_TOKEN_SECRET` used to be documented here and were
#  required in production. Nothing ever read either one. Sessions, CSRF tokens
#  and BYOS worker tokens are long random strings stored only as SHA-256
#  digests, so none of them is signed with a key — which means rotating those
#  variables did nothing, however loudly this file said otherwise. They have
#  been removed rather than quietly left in place; setting them is now ignored.
# ==============================================================================

# base64-encoded 32-byte key for AES-256-GCM encryption of user-supplied
# secrets: BYOK provider keys, MCP server credentials, skill configuration.
# Optional in development, REQUIRED in production.
# WHEN ABSENT (development only): a fixed development key is used.
# WARNING: this key is not rotatable without re-encrypting existing rows —
# changing it makes every stored ciphertext undecryptable.
# ENCRYPTION_KEY=replace_me_openssl_rand_base64_32


# ==============================================================================
#  MODE
# ==============================================================================

# Force demo mode even when real credentials are present.
# Optional — defaults to false. Accepts 1/true/yes/on.
# Set to true for a public playground: all model calls, sandbox operations
# and payments are routed to mocks and nothing can be charged or executed
# for real, regardless of what other keys are set below.
KARO_DEMO_MODE=false

# Show the "Sign in to the demo account" button on the login screen.
# Optional — defaults to true.
# The demo account is created by `npm run db:seed` with SEED_DEMO_PASSWORD.
# Set to false on any install that has real customer data.
KARO_ALLOW_DEMO_LOGIN=true


# ==============================================================================
#  MODEL PROVIDERS
#
#  Every provider Karo talks to speaks OpenAI-compatible Chat Completions, so
#  turning one on means setting one key. Set as many as you like.
#
#  WHEN NONE IS SET: AI_PROVIDER resolves to `mock`. The simulator streams
#  realistic assistant turns, emits tool calls the agent runtime actually
#  executes, and reports plausible token counts, so usage, weighted-token quota
#  and billing all work end to end. Adding any key below switches every model
#  call to that real provider with no other change.
#
#  The registry in src/lib/ai/providers/descriptors.ts is the source of truth
#  for these variable names and for the `auto` preference order.
# ==============================================================================

# Which provider serves model traffic.
#   auto  — (default) the configured provider with the best value ranking
#   mock  — force the simulator even when keys are present
#   or pin one: wandb | openrouter | deepseek | zai | groq | cerebras |
#               siliconflow | together | gemini | mistral | moonshot |
#               anthropic | hypercli | omniakey | ollama
# A pinned provider with no credential does NOT silently fall back at startup:
# Karo logs a warning and the request-time resolver degrades to the simulator.
AI_PROVIDER=auto

# ---- W&B Inference — the default, and the cheapest capable option ------------
# 25 open-weight models on one key, from $0.03/$0.17 per Mtok (gpt-oss-120b) up
# to Qwen3-Coder-480B and GLM-5.2. Verified to support streaming, tool calling
# and per-request usage reporting, which is what Karo's metering needs.
# Free plan carries a $100/month spending cap. Key: https://wandb.ai/authorize
# WANDB_API_KEY=wandb_v1_replace_me
# WANDB_BASE_URL=https://api.inference.wandb.ai/v1

# ---- DeepSeek — the cheapest frontier tier ----------------------------------
# V4-Pro at $0.435/$0.87 per Mtok with a 1M context, roughly a quarter of what
# the same model costs resold. Context caching is automatic and drops repeated
# input ~50x, which matters for an agent that replays a long system prompt.
# Note: inference runs on PRC infrastructure — check that against your own data
# residency commitments before sending customer code.
# DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=sk_replace_me
# DEEPSEEK_BASE_URL=https://api.deepseek.com

# ---- Z.ai (Zhipu GLM) — has a genuinely free model --------------------------
# glm-4.7-flash is $0/$0 with a 200K context and working tool calls: the
# cheapest way to run Karo against a real provider rather than the simulator.
# ZAI_API_KEY=replace_me
# ZAI_BASE_URL=https://api.z.ai/api/paas/v4

# ---- OpenRouter — one key, hundreds of models -------------------------------
# Best when you want to switch model vendors without switching accounts.
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-v1-replace_me
# OPENROUTER_BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1

# ---- Groq / Cerebras — fastest tokens per second, both with free tiers ------
# GROQ_API_KEY=gsk_replace_me
# GROQ_BASE_URL=https://api.groq.com/openai/v1
# CEREBRAS_API_KEY=csk-replace_me
# CEREBRAS_BASE_URL=https://api.cerebras.ai/v1

# ---- SiliconFlow — a wide open-model catalogue at the lowest prices ---------
# Comparable breadth to OpenRouter for open weights, at some of the lowest
# per-token prices anywhere, with $1 of signup credit to try it on.
# SILICONFLOW_API_KEY=replace_me
# SILICONFLOW_BASE_URL=https://api.siliconflow.com/v1

# ---- Together AI — unambiguous model ids ------------------------------------
# Its catalogue publishes an explicit "model string for API" column, so the id
# you copy is the id Karo sends. Prices sit mid-pack.
# TOGETHER_API_KEY=replace_me
# TOGETHER_BASE_URL=https://api.together.ai/v1

# ---- Google Gemini — long context, via the OpenAI-compatible layer ----------
# This is Google's compatibility endpoint, not the native generateContent API.
# Do NOT point customer traffic at the free tier: Google's terms let it train on
# unpaid-tier content and have humans review it. The paid tier does not.
# Key: https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
# GEMINI_API_KEY=replace_me
# GEMINI_BASE_URL=https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/openai

# ---- Mistral — EU-hosted ----------------------------------------------------
# Jurisdiction is the reason to pick it: the cheapest frontier options above run
# on PRC infrastructure. codestral is its code model.
# MISTRAL_API_KEY=replace_me
# MISTRAL_BASE_URL=https://api.mistral.ai/v1

# ---- Moonshot (Kimi) --------------------------------------------------------
# The adapter omits `temperature` and sends `max_completion_tokens` for this
# provider, as its reference requires.
# MOONSHOT_API_KEY=sk-replace_me
# MOONSHOT_BASE_URL=https://api.moonshot.ai/v1

# ---- Anthropic — the Claude family first-hand -------------------------------
# Native Messages protocol (x-api-key + anthropic-version), first-class tool
# calling and exact cache accounting. Published prices: Opus 5 $5/$25,
# Sonnet 5 $3/$15, Fable 5 $10/$50 per Mtok. Key: https://console.anthropic.com
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-replace_me
# ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=https://api.anthropic.com

# ---- HyperCLI — flat-rate Kimi K3 -------------------------------------------
# Daily token allowance (25M-100M tokens/day) instead of per-token billing.
# Served on the Anthropic Messages route, which its docs recommend for tool
# calling. Base URL must stay at the bare host — Karo appends /v1/messages.
# HYPERCLI_API_KEY=hyper_api_replace_me
# HYPERCLI_BASE_URL=https://api.hypercli.com

# ---- Ollama — local models, zero cost ---------------------------------------
# Only activates when this URL is set explicitly, so an install without Ollama
# running does not claim to have a working provider.
# OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1

# ---- Omniakey — reseller of the closed frontier models ----------------------
# Discounted Claude / GPT / Gemini / Grok. No longer the default: for most work
# the open models above are 3x-20x cheaper per token.
# OpenAI-compatible API base URL. Optional — defaults to the value below.
OMNIAKEY_BASE_URL=https://api.omniakey.com/v1

# Public model catalogue used to sync names, context windows and prices.
# Optional — defaults to https://omniakey.com/models.
OMNIAKEY_MODELS_URL=https://omniakey.com/models

# OMNIAKEY_API_KEY=sk_test_replace_me

# There was an OMNIAKEY_SYNC_INTERVAL_MINUTES here, described as how often "the
# background job" refreshes the catalogue. There is no background job — see the
# README's scheduling section — and nothing read the variable. How stale a
# provider may go before the admin models page says so is an admin setting
# (`catalog.sync_interval_minutes`), which is where that decision belongs.


# ==============================================================================
#  SANDBOX PROVIDERS
#
#  The sandbox is the agent's real computer. Karo resolves a provider at
#  runtime and degrades to a simulation rather than refusing to run.
# ==============================================================================

# Which provider to use: `auto` | `mock` | `local-docker` | `daytona` | `remote-docker`.
# Optional — defaults to `auto`.
# `auto` resolution order:
#     1. Daytona          if DAYTONA_API_KEY *and* DAYTONA_API_URL are set
#     2. local-docker     if DOCKER_SOCKET or DOCKER_HOST is set
#     3. mock             otherwise
# KARO_DEMO_MODE=true overrides this to `mock` unconditionally.
SANDBOX_PROVIDER=auto

# Path to the Docker daemon socket for the local-docker provider.
# Optional — no default. Typical value: /var/run/docker.sock
# WHEN ABSENT (and DOCKER_HOST unset): `auto` will not select local-docker.
# Setting this grants the app the ability to create containers on this host.
# Do not set it on a node that also serves public traffic — run sandboxes on
# separate workers (see the diagram in docker-compose.prod.yml).
# DOCKER_SOCKET=/var/run/docker.sock

# Remote Docker daemon endpoint, used by the remote-docker provider.
# Optional — no default. Example: tcp://10.0.0.12:2376
# Always use a TLS-authenticated daemon on a private network; an unprotected
# tcp:// daemon is a remote root shell for anyone who can reach the port.
# DOCKER_HOST=tcp://10.0.0.12:2376

# Image every sandbox container is created from.
# Optional — defaults to karo/sandbox-base:1.
# Build it with: docker build -t karo/sandbox-base:1 docker/sandbox-base
SANDBOX_IMAGE=karo/sandbox-base:1

# Docker network sandbox containers are attached to.
# Optional — defaults to karo-sandbox.
# This network is declared `internal: true` in docker-compose.yml, which is
# what denies agent code any network egress and blocks the cloud metadata
# endpoint. Must match the `name:` of that network.
SANDBOX_NETWORK=karo-sandbox

# Daytona control-plane URL and credentials, for the `daytona` provider.
# Optional — no defaults. Both the URL and the key must be set for `auto` to
# select Daytona; setting only one is ignored.
# WHEN ABSENT: `auto` falls through to local-docker, then mock.
# DAYTONA_API_URL=https://app.daytona.io/api
# DAYTONA_API_KEY=dtn_test_replace_me

# Daytona target/region workspaces are created in.
# Optional — no default; Daytona's own default is used.
# DAYTONA_TARGET=eu


# ==============================================================================
#  BRING YOUR OWN SERVER
#
#  BYOS workers run on the customer's own hardware and dial out to Karo, so
#  they need no inbound port and Karo needs no credentials for their machine.
# ==============================================================================

# Mirror to fetch the worker agent from, if you keep one.
# Optional — empty by default, which serves the agent from this deployment's own
# /api/worker/install. That is the recommended setting: the agent then comes
# from the same host the operator is already trusting with their token.
# This used to default to https://get.karo.dev/worker.sh — a domain that has
# never existed, so the install command the UI printed always failed at step one.
# BYOS_INSTALL_SCRIPT_URL=https://mirror.example.com/karo-worker.sh


# ==============================================================================
#  BILLING (Stripe)
# ==============================================================================

# Stripe secret key.
# Optional — no default.
# WHEN ABSENT: BILLING_PROVIDER resolves to `mock`. The mock provider runs a
# complete checkout flow — plan changes apply, top-ups credit the PAYG
# balance, invoices are generated — without any money moving. Every screen
# in the billing section works.
# Use a `sk_test_...` key against Stripe test mode before going live.
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_replace_me

# Signing secret for the /api/webhooks/stripe endpoint.
# Optional — no default.
# WHEN ABSENT: incoming webhooks are REJECTED rather than trusted, because an
# unverified webhook can grant a subscription for free. Get the value from
# `stripe listen --forward-to localhost:3000/api/webhooks/stripe` in
# development, or from the Stripe dashboard's endpoint page in production.
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_test_replace_me


# ==============================================================================
#  EMAIL
# ==============================================================================

# How outbound mail is delivered: `console` | `smtp`.
# Optional — defaults to `console`.
# `console` writes the full message, including verification and password
# reset links, to the server log. That is the intended development flow: no
# mail server, and you copy the link straight out of the terminal.
#
# ANY REAL DEPLOYMENT NEEDS `smtp`. Verification and password-reset links are
# the only route into the product for a user who cannot read your server log,
# so `console` in production means nobody but you can complete sign-up. A
# production process boots with a warning on `console`, and refuses to start at
# all on `smtp` without SMTP_URL below.
EMAIL_TRANSPORT=console

# From header on outbound mail.
# Optional — defaults to `Karo <no-reply@karo.local>`.
# Use a domain you have SPF/DKIM for, or your mail lands in spam.
EMAIL_FROM=Karo <no-reply@karo.local>

# SMTP connection string.
# REQUIRED when EMAIL_TRANSPORT=smtp — no default.
# Anything nodemailer accepts as a URL works. Use the `smtps://` scheme for
# implicit TLS on 465, or `smtp://` on 587 where the server upgrades with
# STARTTLS. Percent-encode any reserved character in the password.
#
#   smtps://apikey:SG.xxxx@smtp.sendgrid.net:465
#   smtp://postmaster%40mg.example.com:pass@smtp.mailgun.org:587
#   smtps://AKIA...:secret@email-smtp.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com:465
#
# Delivery is pooled (3 connections, 100 messages each) and every stage has a
# timeout, so an unreachable relay fails the send rather than hanging the
# request. `/api/health` reports the transport under `email` and verifies the
# connection at most once every five minutes.
#
# A failed send is never downgraded to the console transport: a password-reset
# link is a single-use credential and does not belong in a log aggregator.
# SMTP_URL=smtps://user:pass@smtp.example.com:465


# ==============================================================================
#  SAFETY
# ==============================================================================

# Disable rate limiting entirely.
# Optional — defaults to false.
# Only for load testing and E2E suites that would otherwise trip the limiter.
# Never true in production: the limiter is what stands between one script and
# your whole model budget.
RATE_LIMIT_DISABLED=false

# Extra hostnames the outbound SSRF guard will allow, comma-separated.
# Optional — no default.
# WHEN ABSENT: only the built-in allowlist applies, and requests to private
# ranges, loopback and 169.254.169.254 are refused. This guard covers URLs
# that reach the server from users and from model output — MCP server URLs,
# webhooks, agent fetches — so widen it deliberately and narrowly.
# OUTBOUND_ALLOWED_HOSTS=api.internal.example.com,files.example.com

# How many reverse proxies sit in front of the app.
# Optional — defaults to 1.
# This is a SECURITY setting, not a tuning knob. The client IP behind every
# IP-scoped rate limit (including auth.login) is read this many entries from the
# RIGHT of `X-Forwarded-For`, because a proxy appends the real address and a
# client can forge everything to its left. Set it to the real number of hops:
#   0  — nothing in front of the app. Forwarding headers are then ignored
#        entirely and all callers share one bucket, which is safe but coarse.
#   1  — the shipped topology: one load balancer terminating TLS.
# Setting this too HIGH is the dangerous direction: it starts trusting entries
# the client controls, which restores unlimited password brute-forcing.
TRUST_PROXY_HOPS=1

# Launchers a `stdio` MCP server may use, comma-separated.
# Optional — defaults to `npx,uvx`, which is what every shipped MCP template
# uses.
# `command` and `args` for an MCP server come from a request body and the process
# is spawned on the CONTROL PLANE, so this allow-list is what stops a member with
# `mcp.manage` from executing arbitrary binaries on the Karo host. An empty value
# disables stdio MCP servers entirely — the right choice for a multi-tenant
# deployment, since `npx <package>` can still fetch and run any published
# package. Remote (streamable HTTP / SSE) MCP servers are unaffected.
# MCP_STDIO_ALLOWED_COMMANDS=npx,uvx

# Shared secret that lets a scheduler call maintenance endpoints without a
# browser session.
# REQUIRED in production — no default.
#
# The `scheduler` service in docker-compose.prod.yml reads this and refuses to
# start without it. Three behaviours Karo bills for depend on that service:
#
#   */5  * * * *  /api/admin/sandboxes/sweep     idle sandboxes are slept and
#                                                expired ones destroyed
#   */10 * * * *  /api/cron/billing/auto-topup   balances refill for teams that
#                                                asked; nothing else can, since
#                                                an empty team meters nothing
#   17   * * * *  /api/cron/billing/apply-pending downgrades parked to the end of
#                                                a paid period actually land
#
# WHEN ABSENT: all three routes fall back to accepting a platform-admin browser
# session only. Nothing breaks visibly — an operator can still press the buttons
# in /admin — but idle sandboxes are billed for the whole idle window, an
# exhausted balance never recovers on its own, and a scheduled downgrade stays
# pending forever. These are silent revenue and trust bugs, not outages.
#
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
# CRON_SECRET=replace_me_openssl_rand_base64_32

# Minimum log level: `debug` | `info` | `warn` | `error`.
# Optional — defaults to `info`.
# `debug` logs full agent tool arguments and results. Those are redacted for
# secrets, but they are still user content — keep it off in production.
LOG_LEVEL=info

# Where to forward errors, in addition to the log.
# Optional — no default.
#
# Any URL that accepts a JSON POST: a Slack or Discord incoming webhook, Better
# Stack, an OTEL collector's HTTP receiver, a three-line Lambda. Karo ships no
# error-tracking SDK on purpose — picking a vendor for an install you may resell
# is not the framework's decision, and an SDK that hooks process globals is a
# poor thing to inherit. `src/lib/observability/report.ts` is the seam if you
# would rather call one.
#
# WHEN ABSENT: errors are still logged, with full context, including the ones
# that used to be invisible — uncaught Server Component failures now go through
# `onRequestError`, and every client-side crash is POSTed back by its error
# boundary instead of dying in the visitor's own devtools. You just have to be
# reading the log to notice.
#
# The payload is scrubbed before it leaves the process: bearer tokens, provider
# key shapes, Karo's own session and worker credentials, and passwords inside
# URLs are replaced. Repeats are collapsed to one delivery per fingerprint per
# minute with a suppressed count, because the usual shape of an incident is the
# same error ten thousand times and a chat channel that receives all of them is
# useless exactly when it is needed.
# ERROR_WEBHOOK_URL=https://hooks.slack.com/services/T000/B000/xxxx


# ==============================================================================
#  SEED
#
#  Used only by `npm run db:seed`. Never read at runtime.
# ==============================================================================

# Password for the seeded demo user, the account behind the "Sign in to the
# demo account" button.
# Optional — defaults to `karo-demo-2025`.
# Change it, or set KARO_ALLOW_DEMO_LOGIN=false, on any internet-facing install.
SEED_DEMO_PASSWORD=karo-demo-2025

# Email address of the seeded platform admin (full /admin access).
# Optional — defaults to admin@karo.local.
SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@karo.local

# Password for that admin account.
# Optional — defaults to `karo-admin-2025`.
# This one really does need changing before the install is reachable from
# anywhere but your laptop.
SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD=karo-admin-2025
